PCI: mediatek: serialise the demultiplexing handler against itself

mtk_pcie_intr_handler() is installed as a chained handler, so it runs without
the serialisation genirq applies to an ordinary one: no descriptor lock, no
in-progress flag.  That is safe only while the parent interrupt is delivered
to a single CPU, and not every controller can promise it.

On the EcoNet EN751221 the parent is a cascade line shared by both hardware
threads of the core, and its controller keeps no per-thread state, so both
threads enter this handler at the same time.  They race on the read then
write-one-to-clear of PCIE_INT_STATUS, and because the INTx latch is edge
triggered the loser clears a status bit whose device interrupt is never
delivered: handle_simple_irq() finds the descriptor already in progress and
dummy_irq_chip provides no resend, so the event is simply dropped.  Once that
happens to a card that is waiting on it, the driver blocks -- an mt76 radio on
this board loses its firmware reply and reports "MCU message timed out",
followed by a hardware restart it cannot complete.

Take a per-port raw spinlock across the handler so the two dispatches are
ordered rather than overlapping.  It costs nothing where the parent already
lands on one CPU: the lock is then uncontended.

While here, bound the MSI drain loop.  A status bit that cannot be cleared
would otherwise spin there forever with interrupts disabled.

--- a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c
@@ -202,6 +202,7 @@
  * @inner_domain: inner IRQ domain
  * @lock: protect the msi_irq_in_use bitmap
  * @msi_irq_in_use: bit map for assigned MSI IRQ
+ * @hw_irq_lock: serialise the demultiplexing handler against itself
  */
 struct mtk_pcie_port {
 	void __iomem *base;
@@ -220,6 +221,7 @@
 	struct irq_domain *irq_domain;
 	struct irq_domain *inner_domain;
 	struct mutex lock;
+	raw_spinlock_t hw_irq_lock;
 	DECLARE_BITMAP(msi_irq_in_use, MTK_MSI_IRQS_NUM);
 };
 
@@ -693,6 +695,21 @@
 
 	chained_irq_enter(irqchip, desc);
 
+	/*
+	 * A chained handler carries none of the serialisation genirq gives a
+	 * normal one: no descriptor lock, no in-progress flag. That is fine
+	 * while the parent interrupt reaches a single CPU, but not every
+	 * controller can promise it -- on the EN751221 the parent is a shared
+	 * cascade line and both hardware threads enter here at once. They then
+	 * race on the read-then-write-one-to-clear below, and since the INTx
+	 * latch is edge triggered, the loser clears a status bit whose device
+	 * interrupt is never delivered: handle_simple_irq() finds the descriptor
+	 * already in progress and dummy_irq_chip offers no resend, so the event
+	 * is dropped for good. Take a lock of our own so the two dispatches are
+	 * ordered instead of overlapping.
+	 */
+	raw_spin_lock(&port->hw_irq_lock);
+
 	status = readl(port->base + PCIE_INT_STATUS);
 	if (status & INTX_MASK) {
 		for_each_set_bit_from(bit, &status, PCI_NUM_INTX + INTX_SHIFT) {
@@ -714,14 +731,21 @@
 			 * be cleared before being dispatched to the
 			 * handler of the underlying device.
 			 */
+			int loops = MTK_MSI_IRQS_NUM * 2;
+
 			writel(MSI_STATUS, port->base + PCIE_INT_STATUS);
-			while ((imsi_status = readl(port->base + PCIE_IMSI_STATUS))) {
+			/* Bounded: a status bit we cannot clear would otherwise
+			 * spin here forever with interrupts off. */
+			while ((imsi_status = readl(port->base + PCIE_IMSI_STATUS)) &&
+			       loops--) {
 				for_each_set_bit(bit, &imsi_status, MTK_MSI_IRQS_NUM)
 					generic_handle_domain_irq(port->inner_domain, bit);
 			}
 		}
 	}
 
+	raw_spin_unlock(&port->hw_irq_lock);
+
 	chained_irq_exit(irqchip, desc);
 }
 
@@ -733,6 +757,8 @@
 	struct platform_device *pdev = to_platform_device(dev);
 	int err;
 
+	raw_spin_lock_init(&port->hw_irq_lock);
+
 	err = mtk_pcie_init_irq_domain(port, node);
 	if (err) {
 		dev_err(dev, "failed to init PCIe IRQ domain\n");
